ansgenic mice  while applying the ultrasound and trying  to see what's going on and in vitro  we're working with cultured neurons  where we can again look with imaging at  what the ultrasound is doing to them and  we can have much more access to perturb  them in various ways pharmacologically  genetically etc to try to get at some of  these mechanism I'm going to give you a  summary of what we found in both of  these cases so first of all looking at  cortical activation in mice in response  to ultrasound so what the experiment  we're doing is taking skull fins Mouse  preparations doing wide field gcamp  six calcium imaging and applying  ultrasound to the visual cortex of the  animal this is a mouse that's under  under light anesthesia and we were  hoping we were hoping to see was a  response in the region that we're  targeting with ultrasound but to our  surprise what we found instead is that  there was a lot of activation of the  auditory regions which were not directly  targeted by our ultrasound giel and you  can see that in these images here the  auditory regions are outlined in the top  of the slide and then in the bottom part  of the slide we're looking at that as a  function of intensity and time and the  little circle and the bottom images  outlines where we're applying the  ultrasound and so what you can see there  is you get instead of direct activation  at the Austin focus you were getting  this contralateral biased activation of  the auditory cortex and if you apply a  large enough intensity and wait long  enough yes you will also see activation  of the visual cortex but it wasn't clear  that this this activation was happening  directly or was arising as a byproduct  of auditory cortex activation and so if  you look at this in a little bit more  detail over over multiple animals you  can see consistently that you get  contralateral auditory cortex activation  that is happening to a higher amplitude  and earlier then you get visual cortex  stimulation at the target and there's a  contralateral bias meaning that when  we're stimulating on the right side of  the mouse brain we were getting more  activation in the left auditory cortex  so this made us think that you know  maybe there's some kind of auditory  activation and this was a little bit  surprising because you know ultrasound  by definition is operating in  frequencies that we shouldn't be able to  hear and the frequencies that we're  using here should be inaudible to mice  however perhaps there's some kind of  conversion that's happening or there's  something that kind of frequencies that  are part of our stimulation parameters  that are not just all sound frequencies  but could be audible to these animals  and so to test that we compared the  cortical response to our ultrasound  shown in the middle row there to light  showing above and to sound shown below  and you can see that when we apply  flashes of light this is to the eyes we  see very nice visual cortex activation  but when we apply both ultrasound and  sound we see this pattern of activation  that corresponds to auditory activation  and the fact that the ultrasound  response and the audible sound response  played through speakers on the outside  looks similar suggests that indeed there  is an auditory side effect that is  at play here and we can actually compare  quantitatively the similarity in the  quartet cortical response between the  ultrasound versus sound and light and  show quantitatively that the light hat  looks dissimilar from both of the other  two whereas the ultrasound and sound  look very similar to each other in terms  of the effect on the cortical activation  and that also made us wonder whether  some of the movement responses that are  seen when you apply ultrasound to  animals could be due to these auditory  effects rather than direct stimulation  of the motor cortex and the idea is that  in these lightly an anesthetized animals  there could be a startle response to a  strong sensation and so to test for that  we measured EMG signals in these mice  while applying ultrasound as well as  light sound and an air puff to the front  of the face as a non auditory non visual  stimulus and what we saw is that while  light Andy's anesthetized animals was  not eliciting a mechanical response  audible sound ultrasound and air puff or  eliciting similar responses and that  made us think that perhaps this is a  startle type of reflex now to test  whether the auditory the ultrasound is  producing this kind of startle response  through an auditory effect we tried to  deafen these animals chemically and so  here we're doing administration of  kanamycin and if you're awesome I'd wear  that produces a partial deafening and we  hypothesize that when we do that the  responses to sound would be diminished  if the response to ultrasound is  happening through auditory pathways the  response to ultrasound should also be  diminished but the response to air puffs  should not be affected because that's  not happening through this deafening  susceptible pathway and indeed that  turned out to be the case so when we  look at the deafened animals they had a  reduced response to both sound and  ultrasound but not to air puffs and so  that makes us believe that we that we  have this auditory on a side effect  that's happening in these mice and while  we were doing this study there was a  complimentary study that was happening  at University of Minnesota led by hunks  on grow in Hubert limbs lab that was  published back-to-back with our paper  where they did similar experiments in  guinea pigs and in this case they were  doing electrophysiology recording from  the auditory cortex while applying  ultrasound to various places in the  brain and also not even in the brain for  example on the eye of the animal and  they saw responses that they could  record regardless of where they were  stimulating which also suggested that  there might be a an auditory side effect  happening here and to show that this was  happening through on three mechanisms  they did a very elegant experiment where  they drained the cochlear fluid from the  ear of these animals and showed that the  responses disappeared so that the things  that they were looking at were also  likely to have been these auditory  effects so you know that was kind of a a  you know somewhat depressing  instead of findings for us because we  were hoping that you know just be simple  and direct etc but you know I think in  retrospect there's good reason to think  that this should have been detectable  and that's because the post parameters  that were used in both of these studies  and in fact were used in the majority of  the published work on ultrasonic near  modulation to that date were repetitive  ultrasound stimulation where the  ultrasound is being applied at hundreds  of kilohertz frequency that's inaudible  but the pulses are being repeated at a  repetition frequency of for example a  kilohertz which is in the audible range  and so if you take a Fourier transform  of that waveform then you're gonna see  not just your ultrasound frequency but  also the pulse repetition frequency  which could cause these auditory effects  and even if you are creating a  continuous wave pattern that should be a  pure tone the abrupt  turning on and turning off of that  pattern can also lead to the generation  of additional frequencies besides your  pure tone frequency and so that's  something that we're trying to  investigate to a greater depth as I'll  talk about in a few slides now at the  same time as we were doing this in vivo  investigation and getting these kind of  unwelcome let's say results we're also  doing in vitro experiments and in this  case we're working with cortical neurons  from mice that are cultured on  acoustically transparent substrates and  in 3-dimensional hydrogel culture or  obviously there's no auditory system so  any direct that we should be seeing from  ultrasound so I think that these neurons  should be direct effects and this is  work led by some genu a postdoctoral  fellow in our lab and what we saw here  again using optical imaging to look at  this at these responses were nice robust  responses so we see these calcium  responses both in two-dimensional and  three-dimensional culture that scale as  expected with intensity and post  duration using parameters that match the  upper end of what has been used in in  vivo studies and so this suggests that  there's in addition to any kind of  auditory side effect that there's also a  direct effect that ultrasound can have  on these neurons and we've been studying  the mechanisms by which that is  happening and and what I want to show  here is just a few things that it's not  so it's not pore forming so after  ultrasound stimulation the neurons  continue to be intact so those times not  is not forming pores and it's not a  thermal mechanism we can measure the  temperature changes and they are less  than a tenth of a degree Celsius it is  not a synaptic phenomenon we can block  synapses and continue to see the same  kind of responses and it probably does  not involve cavitation so for this we  have what's shown here are some voltage  recordings where we're seeing the  depolarization and if there were a  cavitation there was a prediction that  there should be hyper-polarization  happening as well  Nishan by looking at ultra-high frame  rate video recordings of these neurons  as they're being exposed to ultrasound  we don't really see any kind of bubbles  so at least there aren't things that are  visible at the micron type of level that  are involved so this really seems to be  some kind of direct mechanical  interaction with with neurons and we're  we're just finishing up a story about  what mechanosensitive receptors are  involved in mediating that interaction  so where we are with this director or  sonic neuromodulation is that there  seems to be an auditory side effect that  is present in addition to whatever kind  of direct effects are there and some of  the motor responses that we and others  see may be due to this auditory side  effect rather than direct your  modulation so that means that we really  need to look inside the brain to see  what the ultrasound is doing at the same  time our in-vitro results show that we  have robust direct excitation of the  neurons it doesn't seem to be toxic and  it involves specific molecular  mechanisms so there is also a direct  modulation that's happening and so the  next phase of our work and for others  working in this field is to figure out  what are what is the set of parameters  that we can use acoustically to minimize  the auditory side effects while  maximizing the direct neuromodulation  and hopefully we can get as much of that  separation as possible and then for  whatever remains design proper shamp  controls that would be needed to  overcome these auditory effects and we  have continuing NH brain initiative  funded project that involves several  people the investigators listed on the  slide where we're continuing to work in  transgenic mice in cultured neurons  we're doing computational modeling to  see how the ultrasound field gets  converted into other frequencies shear  waves etc to lead to potential auditory  effects and direct effects and human  psychophysical experiments to try to  document what human subjects would be  experiencing and how we can how we can  design good sham controls to make this  really useful technology so that's what  I want to say about the direct  modulation and now I want to  talk about how we can bring this to a  more kind of molecular engineered cell  type-specific level and the idea is that  you know for trying to study the  activity of specific brain regions or  we're trying to treat a disease it's  often important not only to control the  location in the brain where we're trying  to modulate something and the timing of  what we're trying to do it but also to  address specific cell types because at  any given millimeter sized volume that  we're targeting with our ultrasound  they're going to be multiple cell types  they're they're involved in different  processes and pathways and the ideal  technology for newer modulation would  allow us to non-invasively control  location timing and cell types and the  technology we're working on to allow us  to do that is something that we call  acoustically targeted chemo genetics and  unsurprisingly the location targeting  here is being done with focused  ultrasound because of the advantages  that I talked about earlier except here  instead of direct modulation at the time  of those sounds being applied the  function of the ultrasound is to locally  open the blood-brain barrier to allow  the transiently allow the entry of  nanometer sized particles into that part  of the brain and the particles were  introducing our viral vectors that enter  the brain just at that location and are  excluded elsewhere by the BBB and they  can deliver a genetic payload under cell  type-specific promoters and the genetic  payload in this case our team and  genetic receptors that can be activated  by chemo genetic ligands like the CNO  ligand that's shown here and so the idea  is that we can combine these three  things together to give us access to a  particular brain lesion specific cell  types than those region with timing that  we can control through the chemo genetic  ligand administration and I just want to  go briefly through each of these  components so first of all focused also  on blood-brain barrier opening is a  technology that's been developed over  the last 20 25 years by several groups  that has made really amazing progress  and we're following in their footsteps  and the key idea here is that normally  the BBB comprises  tight junctions that prevent most  proteins and nanoparticles entering from  circulation into the brain but in this  case we administer microbubbles  fda-approved  or some contrast agents that go through  the blood and wherever we're applying  the focused ultrasound with particular  parameters that we're using we cause  these bubbles to expand and contract and  push against the endothelial wall and  this kind of massaging action causes the  tight junctions to open just the crack  to now allow particles that are a few up  to a few tens of nanometers in size to  enter into the brain and after a few  hours these tight junctions close back  up and this is something that has been  worked on for a number of years and  actually recently was demonstrated very  elegantly in humans I'm showing some  data on the bottom right of the slide  here from the nature Communications  paper that sighted there at the bottom  where that is a human brain and the  little white area there a little white  rectangle is something that where the  older sound was used to open the  blood-brain barrier at that location and  an MRI contrast agent that's normally  BBB impermeable was able to enter and  provide that context so this is a  technique that works all the way from  small animal models to to human subjects  now the particles that we're introducing  after opening the BBB  Rav viral vectors because they're small  and you can use them to transfer  clearance people do it all the time with  intracranial injections and you can put  in your gene of interest under a cell  type specific promoter if one is  available and we'd like a V for those  considerations and also because recently  they were one of the first types of  viral vectors to be approved for gene  therapy by the FDA and the payload that  we're introducing are chemo genetic  receptors I think most of this audience  is going to be familiar with them really  a beautiful technology in which you  modify a receptor like a GPCR to stop  responding to its native  neurotransmitter and instead respond to  a designer drug and in our case we're  using this class of receptors called  dreads that was  by Brian Roth in his group that respond  to  clozapine and oxide CNO as well as other  new ligands that that people have  developed and so the idea I'm putting  this all together is that we do a brief  focused on procedure where we apply the  ultrasound to the brain region that we  want to control open the BBB inject  systemically Rav viral vectors encoding  dreads under cell type-specific promoter  let them enter the brain at that  location wait a couple weeks for  expression to take hold and subsequently  whenever we want to control that part of  the brain we just give the chemo genetic  ligand and the beauty of this from my  perspective is that we only have to do  the ultrasound watts and it's a 15  minute procedure and then from then on  you don't have to walk around with an  ultrasound transducer on your head to  achieve near modulation you just have to  take a systemically available small  molecule and conversely the small  molecule therapy works like a normal  pharmacological treatment except instead  of that small molecule acting on all  parts of the brain that may have the  receptor for it and acting in a way that  produces side-effects now this drug is  only going to be acting on the part of  the brain and only on the neurons and  and the cell types that we selected we  targeted through our ultrasound gene  delivery procedure so how well does this  work well initially we did this in mice  and we wanted to control the mouse  hippocampus one of the nice things about  focused ultrasound even compared to  intracranial injections is that to cover  a certain region you don't have to do  multiple discreet injections you can  just sweep the ultrasound beam in paint  the pattern of gene expression that you  want and so in this case we're doing  that in the mouths of the campus and we  can check immediately after the  procedure using an MRI contrast agent  where we've opened the BBB so we can see  here that we were successful in opening  it at the hippocampus and then we inject  our viral vectors in this case we have a  fluorophore  our dread receptor that we can tag and  then we can look a few weeks later where  we got our expression and you can see  here that we have nicely transfected the  important parts of the hippocampus and  we get transfection levels that are on  the order of 50 percent of cells at the  doses of virus that were administering  here and we can also make the cells that  cell type selective for example  targeting transfection to only  excitatory neurons and to show that this  works we perform the behavioral  experiments with fear conditioning in  mice where the idea was that we used  acoustically targeted Kim's genetics to  put in inhibitory dreads into excitatory  neurons in the hippocampus such that if  we administer CNO before my salmon ago  our fear conditioning we would suppress  memory formation and subsequent fear  recall in the mice that receive this you  know and not in the mice that either  didn't receive the attack treatment or  didn't receive the chemo genetic ligand  before undergoing fewer training and  indeed that turned out to be the case so  you can see in the plot on the left here  that we're able to suppress Theo recall  as measured by context freezing in the  recall task by more than a factor of two  and this magnitude of this effect is  comparable to what people have seen  previously with chemo genetic and  optogenetic treatments so in this case  we were able to implement this  technology successfully in mouse model  so the advantages from our point of view  is that this is a fully non-surgical  procedure can you apply the ultrasound  only once you can cover larger brain  regions by sweeping it around which  becomes even more important as you scale  this to larger animals as we'll talk  about in a moment this technology works  through well-established  mechanisms in the sense that the  blood-brain barrier opening has been  worked on for some time viral vectors  have been worked on for some time Hakeem  genetics has been worked on for some  time and so we're combining those  established techniques together and we  don't have to wonder  about you know how how is this all  working like we do with a direct  ultrasonic ear modulation of course the  downside is that we don't you know  control things on this kind of  millisecond or second timescale that you  could in principle do with direct  modulation but instead we're modulating  things on the time scale of the  pharmacokinetics of the chemo genetic  ligand but you know I think for a lot of  experiments and potential therapeutic  approaches that time scale is adequate  and also gives us some room to play with  because people are always developing new  chemo genetic receptors and and ligands  and so where we are with that work is  developing attack 2.0 this is an NIH  funded project from the brain initiative  that we're very excited about where we  are trying to scale this technology to  non-human primates and we think that as  a system in which this technique could  really shine because it's otherwise  difficult to deliver viral vectors into  kind of the size and distribution of  brain regions that we and others are  interested in doing in this type of  animal model now to do that we're trying  to enhance all the different aspects of  our system for example we are  engineering the viral sera types that  we're using in mice we used a benign  where we'd like to develop new serotypes  that are even better at entering the  brain at the location of ultrasonic  blood-brain barrier opening and I do  less in terms of transfecting other  parts of the body even though we're  using them under cell type-specific  promoters and this would give us a  better therapeutic window and allow us  to use a smaller dose of these viral  vectors we're testing new chemo genetic  reagents that a lot of groups have been  publishing that are very exciting and  we're going to be adapting them into  this into the system and trying to  develop inexpensive ultrasound arrays  for use in non-human primates the  devices that I showed when introducing  focused ultrasound that are used in  humans for ablation are very expensive  and probably not something that would  get disseminated widely in the  our science community but there are some  technologies that would allow us to make  that much less expensive because our  goal is to make this that's gonna be  something that's going to be useful for  non-human primate another animal model  research as broadly as possible and  doing all of these things required as a  multidisciplinary team and so we have  the list of investigators myself  Doris Sound Viviana Grandin ro at Cal  Tech who are working on the viral  engineering and implementation initial  implementation in non-human primates  Charles caskey will Grissom and lumen  Chen at Vanderbilt who are experts in  focused ultrasound and using MRI to  visualize and control that ultrasound as  well as non-human primate neuroscience  which will allow us to validate our  approach in two different labs to  different animal models to make this  really a robust technology and this is  something that this very wealthy  disciplinary project is something that  is specifically enabled by the brain  initiative and we're very grateful for  that  for that support so to summarize you  know I talked about this bottom left  quadrant here where we can use sound  waves to control neural activity and I  showed a couple of different ways of  doing that the broader statement that  I'd like to make for people that are  interested in this field is that  recently it's also becoming possible to  use ultrasound to visualize neural  activity and in technology that's  available already today you can do that  through human dynamics with functional  ultrasound develop developed by Mikhail  tantor and colleagues in Paris and we  are also developing molecular tools that  allow us to look at things like gene  expression and potentially more dynamic  signals using u